  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nToday through Thursday Night (Winter Storm Potential)... A pair\nof cutoff lows over the northern and southern plains early this\nmorning to have the northern one deepen into the Ozarks by\nmidnight tonight with the southern one moving to the FL\npanhandle. Surface low over the northern Gulf has an inverted\ntrof extending northward across the western slopes of the\nAppalachians today and pivoting nw into IL overnight. Isolated\nlight showers to develop/spread northward over southeast and\neast central IL during this morning, while main band of\nprecipitation spreads northward over areas from I-57 west \nduring mid to late afternoon and evening. Strong frontogenesis \nwill intensify tonight with this approaching trof and will see \ndynamic cooling as well with this stronger forcing. Thermal \nprofiles supporting snow over west central/sw IL where best \nchances of seeing snow accumulations, as much as 3-6 inches from\nJacksonville sw. NBM guidance indicates a 10-20% chance of \nsnowfall rates of 1 inch or more per hour at times over Scott \ncounty this evening. Later runs of HRRR and RRFS models showing \na dry wedge into central IL overnight with NE flow with highest \npops in southern/se IL and sw and west central IL. Highs today \nin the lower 40s, with some upper 30s sw CWA. Lows tonight in \nthe low to mid 30s, coolest over sw CWA. \n\nModels trends continue to shift higher snowfall amounts further\nsw this afternoon and tonight and now the highest amounts over 6\ninches are south and sw of Scott and Morgan counties. And areas\nne of Canton to Springfield to Taylorville appear to have less\nthan 1 inch of snow. The evening shift converted the winter\nstorm watch into a winter weather advisory with Scott and Morgan\ncounties upgraded to a winter storm warning.    \n\nNot the nicest weather on Thanksgiving Day for Illinois as the\ndouble barrel cutoff lows over the mid MS river valley and\nCarolinas at dawn Thursday merge into one in eastern KY/TN \nearly Thu evening. Widespread rain expected over central and \nsoutheast IL though west central IL will likely see wintry mix \nThu morning, especially early. The winter weather headlines\ncurrently go until 6 pm Thu over west central IL, but may end\nearlier. Precipitation chances will be lower over northern/nw \nCWA Thu morning, but increase Thu afternoon. High Thu in the \nlower 40s, and blustery north to NW winds gusting 20-30 mph to \nmake it feel even colder. High chances of rain continue Thu \nevening, then diminish from the west overnight Thu night into \nFri morning as storm system pulls away. As temperatures drop \nThursday night behind the departing system, precipitation may \nbriefly mix with or change back to light snow overnight Thu \nnight into Fri morning before ending, though any additional \naccumulation will be minor.\n\nThanksgiving Weekend into Middle of New Week... Large storm \nsystem to eject northeast into New England late Friday taking \nmuch of its moisture east of IL by Friday afternoon/evening.\nHighs Friday in the low to mid 40s with breezy nw winds Fri\nmorning and gradually diminishing during Fri afternoon and\nturning westerly. Upper level flow to become more semi-zonal, \nwith temperatures moderating back to near seasonable levels \nSaturday through next Wednesday. Forecast models in fair \nagreement on a fast moving clipper system moving eastward \nthrough the area overnight Saturday night and Sunday, which \nlikely could return rain showers to the region. GFS shows \nanother upper level trof with moisture into IL on Tue though \nECMWF and GEM deterministic models are keeping IL dry and NBM \npops remain below 20% on Tue. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Dec\n2-8th has a 70-80% chance of above normal temperatures and a \n40-50% chance of above normal precipitation over Illinois. So a\nwetter weather pattern next two weeks may help with the drought\nfrom the IL river southeast.",
